{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Crusader states during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab yet he eventually secured his freedom to rule his inherited lands with considerable skill. He became known for his diplomatic approach toward Muslim neighbors and this strategy often put him at odds with more aggressive factions inside the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond spent roughly ten years as a prisoner of Nur ad-Din following his capture in 1164 while leading forces against Aleppo. This long period of confinement shaped his worldview and fostered a deep understanding of Islamic politics and culture. His release came only after a substantial ransom was paid by the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. The experience made him cautious about unnecessary warfare and he preferred negotiation over direct military confrontation whenever possible.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count acted as regent for Baldwin IV during the young king minority and later for Baldwin V after the death of his father. His tenure as regent faced intense opposition from the court faction led by Agnes of Courtenay and her brother Joscelin III. These rivals accused Raymond of seeking personal power rather than serving the crown interests. Despite these accusations he managed the kingdom affairs with prudence during a time of great external threat from Saladin. His political maneuvering aimed to preserve the fragile state of the Crusader holdings through alliances and treaties rather than reckless campaigns.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army suffered a catastrophic defeat. Some historians argue that his absence from the final planning stages contributed to the poor decisions made by Guy of Lusignan. His death marked the end of an era defined by pragmatic diplomacy in the Levant. The loss of his wise counsel left the kingdom vulnerable to the unified forces of Saladin who soon captured Jerusalem. Raymond remains a complex figure in medieval history because he balanced Christian loyalty with realistic assessments of Muslim strength. His life story illustrates the difficulties faced by Latin lords trying to maintain their position in a hostile environment without constant warfare. The strategies he employed might have saved the kingdom had others followed his example of caution and foresight instead of pursuing glory through battle.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.8253e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0024328595}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.019564148}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.027731227}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.5033983e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0007236834}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.069994725}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.02382438}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.008291489}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.